{"schema_version":"1.9.2","id":"GHSA-8474-xrp6-wh69","published":"2026-11-10T21:34:11Z","modified":"2026-11-10T21:45:04.323273690Z","aliases":["CVE-2026-126080"],"summary":"Active Support: Possible DoS vulnerability in Active Support safe_constantize","details":"### Impact\nWhen constant lookup raises `LoadError`, `ActiveSupport::Inflector#safe_constantize` (and `String#safe_constantize`) builds a regular expression from the requested constant name without escaping it, so a caller that passes untrusted input lets the attacker choose the source of a pattern that the application compiles. Crafted patterns can trigger defects in Ruby's regular expression compiler, possibly leading to a crash of the server process, and repeated requests could keep the application unavailable.\n\nAction Cable passes the `channel` value of each `subscribe` command to `safe_constantize` before any channel lookup, so in the default configuration an unauthenticated client of the Action Cable endpoint can reach this code.\n\n### Details\nThe check that decides whether `safe_constantize` returns `nil` or re-raises the `LoadError` now matches the first segment of the constant name against the error message as a literal string, and no longer compiles a regular expression. It returns the same result as before for constant names that contain no regular expression syntax.\n\n### Affected applications\nAn application is affected if it meets all of these requirements:\n- Mounts Action Cable, which a new Rails application does by default at `/cable` (`config.action_cable.mount_path`), or otherwise passes untrusted input to `safe_constantize`.\n- Accepts Action Cable connections from untrusted clients. `ApplicationCable::Connection#connect` authenticates nobody unless the application adds a check.\n\n### Workarounds\nApplications that do not use Action Cable can set `config.action_cable.mount_path = nil` to remove the endpoint. Applications that use Action Cable can call `reject_unauthorized_connection` in `ApplicationCable::Connection#connect` for unauthenticated clients, which limits exposure to authenticated users but does not remove it.\n\n### Releases\nThe fixed releases are available at the normal locations.\n\nPatches for those who cannot upgrade at once:\n- 8.1: https://github.com/rails/rails/commit/95aa9e416016524956d4904fed96eaed079580bb.patch\n- 8.0: https://github.com/rails/rails/commit/210ac22d446c8e2625b091e79256806b836eee20.patch\n\n### Versions affected\n\n- activesupport < 8.0.5.2\n- activesupport >= 8.1, < 8.1.4.1\n\n### Credit\nThis issue was responsibly reported by Felix Tscheulin ([flixt](https://hackerone.com/flixt)) through HackerOne researcher [scheja](https://hackerone.com/scheja), and independently by Bucky Wolfe ([Igneous](https://github.com/Igneous)), who provided a proof of concept that crashes the server. It was also found by the Anthropic OSS Scanner (ANT-2026-ZUN5KU3Q).","affected":[{"package":{"name":"activesupport","ecosystem":"RubyGems","purl":"pkg:gem/activesupport"},"ranges":[{"type":"ECOSYSTEM","events":[{"introduced":"8.1.0.beta1"},{"fixed":"8.1.4.1"}]}],"versions":["8.1.0","8.1.0.beta1","8.1.0.rc1","8.1.1","8.1.2","8.1.2.1","8.1.3","8.1.3.1","8.1.4"],"database_specific":{"source":"https://github.com/github/advisory-database/blob/main/advisories/github-reviewed/2026/11/GHSA-8474-xrp6-wh69/GHSA-8474-xrp6-wh69.json"}},{"package":{"name":"activesupport","ecosystem":"RubyGems","purl":"pkg:gem/activesupport"},"ranges":[{"type":"ECOSYSTEM","events":[{"introduced":"0"},{"fixed":"8.0.5.2"}]}],"versions":["1.0.0","1.0.1","1.0.2","1.0.3","1.0.4","1.1.0","1.1.1","1.2.1","1.2.2","1.2.3","1.2.4","1.2.5","1.3.0","1.3.1","1.4.0","1.4.1","1.4.2","1.4.3","1.4.4","2.0.0","2.0.1","2.0.2","2.0.4","2.0.5","2.1.0","2.1.1","2.1.2","2.2.2","2.2.3","2.3.10","2.3.11","2.3.12","2.3.14","2.3.15","2.3.16","2.3.17","2.3.18","2.3.2","2.3.3","2.3.4","2.3.5","2.3.6","2.3.6.pre","2.3.7","2.3.8","2.3.8.pre1","2.3.9","2.3.9.pre","3.0.0","3.0.0.beta","3.0.0.bet